WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:03.930
(bright music)

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.760
- Our goal is really to be able to detect

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.200
reckless and dangerous drivers

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.790
before they get to the intersection.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.160
And to do that, we use
recurrent neural networks

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.440
to actually deploy and train our networks

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.270
in a scaled autonomous vehicle platform

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.410
called the MiniCity.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:17.790
We use real hardware

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.130
but scaled to a 1/10 scale size

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.870
to deploy all of our autonomy

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.120
and to be able to detect when
there's a dangerous driver

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.860
at an intersection.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.180
Typically, there's two paradigms,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.680
you either have full scale vehicles,

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.300
which are pretty dangerous

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.850
and expensive to actually
deploy your algorithms

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.440
or you do it purely in simulation,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.660
which is probably the
most popular these days

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:37.500
in the industry.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.840
We're using a scaled physical platform

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.520
so we can actually really see

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.530
how these work in the real world

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:47.100
interacting with real
hardware, sensors, delays,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.090
the things you actually see

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.760
when you deploy these
autonomous vehicles on the road.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.520
Safety in general is a
really hard thing to monitor

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.110
but what's novel about our approach

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.450
is that we're actually
using the traffic lights

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.280
to observe drivers on the road.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.580
And in doing so, we try and
detect from outside of the car

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.170
whether there's a failure occurring

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.390
and actually warn other
vehicles ahead of time.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.340
We looked at four
different types of failures

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.740
that we actually deploy
on our scaled hardware.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.610
The first one is just random
noise that we add and inject

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.730
into the low level steering
and speed of the vehicle.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.160
And this is to simulate
just a random error

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.320
that happens in your autonomy stack.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.810
Second one is a failure in
the perception of the vehicle

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.240
so how it detects the scenes around it.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.250
The third one, we have a speeding driver,

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.220
so this is to simulate
a reckless human driver

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.160
or just, again, another
failure in the autonomy stack.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.140
And then, finally, we
actually brought human drivers

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.130
into the MiniCity

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.220
so we can actually test
out with real human driving

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.290
to show what a reckless
driver might look like.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.910
We wanna actually look over time,

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.290
can we see the pattern

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:47.550
and really the causal nature

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.040
of whether there's a failure
occurring in the driver.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.410
So RNN lets us really pinpoint,

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.120
what is the state of this driver,

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.550
and using both the current driving style

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.470
of the driver of the vehicle,

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.420
but also the history of the trajectory

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.580
we've seen in the past of the vehicle.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.200
Each of the vehicles has the hardware

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.580
you would find on a full scale car,

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.760
so we have lidar, we have
cameras, we have IMUs,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.710
and we also have the onboard compute.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.180
So we have a computer onboard

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.100
and it's ingesting all that information

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.390
with the software we've developed

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.130
so that they could drive autonomously

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.990
and we can put new neural networks,

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:21.750
new algorithms on our cars to test them.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.298
We go from developing
software on our computers,

00:02:24.298 --> 00:02:27.060
put them on the computers,
on the cars themselves,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.500
they collect sensor data,

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.450
and then it comes up with a decision

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.570
on the actual speed that the
wheels of the cars can turn

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.730
and actually the angle on
the steering of the cars.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:36.563
And kind of the dream

00:02:36.563 --> 00:02:38.910
is we would have these
intelligent intersections

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.610
and traffic lights that
actually could be a safety net

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.260
for if there's some type of failure

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.450
that occurs in your car with the driver

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:46.860
or even with your software.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.510
We're always going to have failures,

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.120
and if we can use the
AI to actually predict

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.250
these types of collisions
before they happen,

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.260
hopefully, we can have
much, much safer roads

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.523
for us to drive on.

